"""Format-specific buying information for commercial landing pages."""
BUYING_GUIDES = {
'folding-cartons': dict(
 title='Custom Folding Carton Boxes & Printing',
 heading='Custom Folding Carton Boxes for Retail Products',
 description='Plan custom printed folding cartons around product fit, paperboard, opening style and artwork. Compare structures and prepare your packaging quote.',
 body='''<section class="section wrap"><div class="sectionhead"><div><h2>Choose the carton around the product.</h2><p>A folding carton is a retail pack, not automatically a protective shipping box. Begin with the complete contents, how the carton will be filled and the way the customer opens it.</p></div></div>
<div class="tablewrap" role="region" aria-label="Folding carton structure decisions" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Structure direction</th><th scope="col">What to compare</th><th scope="col">Sample check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Straight tuck or reverse tuck</td><td>Compare flap orientation against the front panel, product insertion and the intended opening end.</td><td>Check tuck access, flap interference and the packed product in its display orientation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Locking base</td><td>Discuss the base construction when product weight or packing workflow makes bottom support a priority.</td><td>Review assembly and the loaded base with the actual item; the structure name alone is not a load rating.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleeve</td><td>Decide whether the sleeve wraps a product, tray or another box and how the contents stay in place.</td><td>Check sliding clearance, retention and whether information is hidden when assembled.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p class="smallnote">These are specification directions to discuss. Confirm the available construction on your project dieline before approving production.</p></section>
<section class="section softbg"><div class="wrap two"><div><h2>Specify board and print together.</h2><p>Start from the material reference on a selected carton, then confirm board type, thickness and the surfaces to be printed. Do not substitute paper weight for board thickness or assume two boards with the same weight will fold identically.</p><p>Identify the panels carrying important logo colors. Discuss CMYK or spot colors, coating, lamination and any foil or embossing as separate choices. Check finishing near folds, glue areas and small type on the proposed board.</p><a class="textlink" href="/guides/brand-color-across-materials/">Plan brand color across materials</a></div>
<div><h2>Measure the contents before the box.</h2><p>For a bottle or jar, include the cap, pump, label and any insert in the fit review. Send maximum product dimensions and explain whether the quoted carton size is internal or external. Agree the order of length, width and height on the drawing.</p><p>A structural sample should answer fit, insertion, closure and removal questions. If an insert is needed, review it with the carton and the product together. Assess the outer shipping pack separately when the carton will travel through parcel delivery.</p><a class="textlink" href="/guides/internal-vs-external-box-dimensions/">Understand internal and external dimensions</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section wrap"><h2>Prepare a folding carton quotation.</h2><p>Catalog references start at 500 pieces. State the quantity for each size and artwork version rather than combining different cartons into one total. The written quotation confirms the production split, tooling, sample route and freight basis.</p>
<ul class="home-checklist"><li>Product dimensions, weight, orientation and any accessories or insert.</li><li>Carton structure or PHG reference, with the changes you need.</li><li>Board reference, printed panels, colors and finishing priorities.</li><li>Quantity per size and design, artwork status and required sample checks.</li><li>Delivery country, target arrival window and the proposed shipping pack.</li></ul>
<div class="related-links"><a href="/industries/beauty-skincare/">Beauty &amp; skincare packaging</a><a href="/industries/coffee-tea/">Coffee &amp; tea packaging</a><a href="/guides/packaging-quote-checklist/">Packaging quotation checklist</a></div></section>
<section class="section softbg"><div class="wrap faqwrap"><div><h2>Folding carton questions.</h2><p>Resolve the structure before treating the artwork as production-ready.</p></div><div class="faq">
<details><summary>Can the carton protect a glass bottle during shipping?</summary><p>Review the bottle, carton, insert and outer shipping pack as one system. A folding carton on its own does not establish transit protection. Share the packed weight and delivery conditions so the sample and transport checks can be agreed.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do I need a final dieline before sending artwork?</summary><p>You can send an early design for discussion. Place production artwork on the approved dieline after dimensions, folds, glue zones and opening orientation are confirmed. Keep the artwork revision attached to the approved structure.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will a plain sample confirm the final printed appearance?</summary><p>A plain structural sample helps check fit and assembly. It does not approve final color, foil, coating or print registration. Identify which printed proof or reference is needed for each appearance decision.</p></details>
<details><summary>How are flat delivery and assembly agreed?</summary><p>Ask how the selected carton will be supplied, which gluing is completed and which steps your packing team performs. Try the proposed assembly sequence with a sample before confirming the order.</p></details>
</div></div></section>'''),
'stand-up-pouches': dict(
 title='Custom Stand Up Pouches & Printed Packaging',
 heading='Custom Printed Stand Up Pouches for Food & Retail',
 description='Plan custom stand-up pouches around fill volume, gusset, zipper, material and sealing equipment. Review sample checks and request a project-specific quote.',
 body='''<section class="section wrap"><div class="sectionhead"><div><h2>Start with the contents and filling method.</h2><p>A bottom-gusset pouch needs a fit and filling check with your actual product. A fill weight from another product is a reference, not a capacity guarantee for your powder, tea, snacks or pet treats.</p></div></div>
<div class="tablewrap" role="region" aria-label="Stand-up pouch specification decisions" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Decision</th><th scope="col">Information to share</th><th scope="col">What to test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Width, height and gusset</td><td>State the measurement convention, target fill weight or volume, and product density or a filling sample.</td><td>Check usable capacity, headspace, standing stability and the filled pack dimensions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zipper and top seal</td><td>Describe how the pouch is filled and closed, the required opening and any tear notch.</td><td>Check access for the filling tool, product clearance above the zipper and a clean final seal area.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Window and printed panels</td><td>Mark the area that should remain clear and where artwork or a label will sit.</td><td>Review visibility with the product inside and readability on the filled pouch.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bottom-gusset or flat-bottom format</td><td>Compare the intended shelf presentation, base, printed panels and filling workflow.</td><td>Fill both proposed structures if needed; do not transfer dimensions from one format to the other.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p><a class="textlink" href="/guides/stand-up-vs-flat-bottom-pouches/">Compare stand-up and flat-bottom pouches</a></p></section>
<section class="section softbg"><div class="wrap two"><div><h2>Review the complete material structure.</h2><p>Use the selected reference to start the material conversation, then identify what the contents need from the complete pouch. Share sensitivity to moisture, oxygen, light, aroma or puncture, together with storage conditions and intended use.</p><p>A material name or a photograph does not establish barrier performance, food-contact suitability or shelf life. Request documentation and testing appropriate to the actual structure and destination market. Confirm any window, valve or other feature for the selected order rather than assuming it is included.</p><a class="textlink" href="/materials/">Explore material and finish considerations</a></div>
<div><h2>Match the pouch to your packing equipment.</h2><p>Tell us whether filling is manual or by machine, what the product is like during filling and how the top seal is made. Share the opening and sealing requirements from your equipment supplier where available.</p><p>Use a filling trial to review pouch handling, contamination of the sealing area, final seal and closure access. A resealable zipper supports repeated opening; it should not be treated as a substitute for the agreed initial seal.</p><a class="textlink" href="/services/sampling/">Plan a useful pouch sample</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section wrap"><h2>Separate quantity, print route and setup costs.</h2><p>Catalog references start at 500 pieces. This starting quantity does not mean every material, size and printing process is available at the same minimum. Ask which print route is proposed and whether samples, printing setup or tooling are quoted separately.</p>
<ul class="home-checklist"><li>Contents, target fill weight or volume and the product available for a filling trial.</li><li>PHG reference, finished size convention, bottom gusset and required opening.</li><li>Zipper, window, notch or other features to confirm for the selected structure.</li><li>Quantity per size and design, artwork versions and material requirements.</li><li>Filling and sealing method, delivery country and target arrival window.</li></ul>
<div class="related-links"><a href="/industries/food-snacks/">Food &amp; snack packaging</a><a href="/industries/coffee-tea/">Coffee &amp; tea packaging</a><a href="/guides/packaging-quote-checklist/">Prepare your pouch brief</a></div></section>
<section class="section softbg"><div class="wrap faqwrap"><div><h2>Stand-up pouch questions.</h2><p>Approve the filled pack as well as the flat pouch.</p></div><div class="faq">
<details><summary>Can I choose a size from the advertised fill weight?</summary><p>Use it only as a starting point. Different products occupy different volumes at the same weight. Confirm the size with your contents, intended headspace and sealing clearance before approving the pouch.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a zipper make the pouch ready for retail filling?</summary><p>Confirm the complete filling and sealing sequence. The opening must suit the filling tool, and the product should leave the final seal area clean. Review the zipper and first-opening arrangement on a filled sample.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a foil-looking pouch guarantee a particular shelf life?</summary><p>No. Appearance does not identify the full film structure or measured barrier. Shelf-life decisions require evidence for the actual product, package, sealing process and storage conditions.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can several designs share one 500-piece order?</summary><p>List the quantity for each size and artwork version. Ask for the proposed print route, setup costs and minimum per version in the quotation; do not assume different designs can be combined without changing the commercial terms.</p></details>
</div></div></section>'''),
'mailer-boxes': dict(
 title='Custom Mailer Boxes & Printed Packaging',
 heading='Custom Printed Mailer Boxes for Ecommerce Orders',
 description='Plan custom corrugated mailer boxes around internal size, inserts, printed surfaces and packing workflow. Compare sample checks and prepare your project quote.',
 body='''<section class="section wrap"><div class="sectionhead"><div><h2>Build the mailer around the complete order.</h2><p>Start with the products, retail cartons, inserts, wrapping and cards that travel together. A box sized around the bare product may leave no space for the final packing arrangement.</p></div></div>
<div class="tablewrap" role="region" aria-label="Mailer box specification decisions" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Decision</th><th scope="col">What to specify</th><th scope="col">Sample check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Internal dimensions</td><td>Identify length, width and depth, the units and the complete contents in their packing orientation.</td><td>Load the actual set and check clearance, lid closure and product removal.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Board and closure</td><td>Share packed weight, product edges, the delivery route and how the mailer will be closed.</td><td>Check the loaded base, locking tabs and closure through the agreed handling checks.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insert or protective packing</td><td>Identify what holds each item and whether the order has several product combinations.</td><td>Pack the smallest and largest intended combinations and check for movement or pressure points.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Printed surfaces</td><td>Mark the outside, inside lid, base and any areas reserved for labels or tape.</td><td>Review visible artwork on the assembled mailer with the shipping label in place.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p><a class="textlink" href="/guides/internal-vs-external-box-dimensions/">Compare internal and external box dimensions</a></p></section>
<section class="section softbg"><div class="wrap two"><div><h2>Separate retail presentation from parcel protection.</h2><p>A folding carton, rigid gift box and corrugated mailer perform different jobs in an order. Explain which components are inside the mailer and whether it travels on its own or inside another shipping box. Review the complete product and packaging combination rather than assuming a decorative finish provides transit protection.</p><p>Ask which board construction and closure are proposed for the actual packed weight and route. A board name or tidy sample photograph does not establish a transport-performance result. Agree any distribution checks before production approval.</p><a class="textlink" href="/guides/mailer-boxes-vs-shipping-boxes/">Compare mailer boxes and shipping boxes</a></div>
<div><h2>Plan print around the packing bench.</h2><p>Shipping labels and tape can cover exterior artwork. Leave space for those practical elements, and identify the inside panels you want customers to see when opening the box. Mark important logo colors, barcodes and any printed instructions on the approved dieline.</p><p>Review the liner and artwork together. Check small text and color appearance on the selected surface, then inspect the folded sample around corners, locking tabs and folds. State any finishing request separately so it can be reviewed for the proposed construction.</p><a class="textlink" href="/guides/packaging-print-proof/">Choose the right print proof</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section wrap"><h2>Send a brief your packing team can use.</h2><p>Catalog mailer references start at 500 pieces. List quantities per size and printed design, including any different inserts. Ask how the mailer is supplied and assembled, what the sample needs to verify and which packing components are included in the quotation.</p>
<ul class="home-checklist"><li>PHG reference or proposed mailer format, with the changes you need.</li><li>Complete contents, packed weight and internal dimensions with units.</li><li>Insert, wrapping, seal or tape requirements and the packing sequence.</li><li>Printed sides, artwork versions, important colors and label positions.</li><li>Quantity per design, destination, sample priorities and target arrival window.</li></ul>
<div class="related-links"><a href="/industries/ecommerce/">Ecommerce &amp; subscription packaging</a><a href="/products/custom-boxes/box-inserts/">Review box inserts</a><a href="/guides/packaging-quote-checklist/">Prepare your packaging quotation brief</a></div></section>
<section class="section softbg"><div class="wrap faqwrap"><div><h2>Mailer box questions.</h2><p>Check the packed order, the opening experience and the way your team assembles the box.</p></div><div class="faq">
<details><summary>Should I send internal or external dimensions?</summary><p>State which convention you are using and include the actual product measurements. Internal dimensions help define usable packing space; the finished outer parcel must also be checked for storage and shipping planning. Confirm both on the agreed drawing and packed sample.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do all orders need the same insert?</summary><p>List the smallest and largest product combinations and show how each is packed. Review whether one insert can hold those combinations or whether different layouts are needed. Do not approve only the most convenient sample arrangement.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I print the inside as well as the outside?</summary><p>Mark each requested printed panel on your brief. Ask for the proposed print route, surface and cost for that layout, then review the assembled proof. Do not assume both sides are included because a reference image shows interior artwork.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will a mailer be supplied ready to fill?</summary><p>Confirm how the selected construction is delivered and which assembly, insert and sealing steps your team performs. Try the complete packing sequence with a sample before confirming a repeatable fulfilment workflow.</p></details>
</div></div></section>''')
}
